Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show $\Kempirical \leq 2$ across all 10 model configurations: 6/10 configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Qwen with 4 configs, LLaMA-8B, and Pythia-1.4B, spanning three of six families) (1v2 $\auc$ range: 0.6074--0.6955; five of six CI lower bounds $> \theta$; permutation $p < 0.001$), while OLMo-1B, Mistral-7B, Pythia-6.9B, and Gemma-2-2B yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ (1v2 $\auc$: 0.500, 0.594, 0.596, and 0.598, all below $\theta$; Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}).
Under Bonferroni correction for 10 simultaneous comparisons (99.5\% CIs), three of six boundary configurations retain CI lower bounds above $\theta$; the three most marginal cases, Pythia-1.4B (0.593), Qwen-continuation (0.598), and LLaMA-8B (0.599), fall below the widened interval.
All three retain point estimates above $\theta$; Qwen-continuation and LLaMA-8B also retain uncorrected CI lower bounds above $\theta$, while Pythia-1.4B (0.598) falls marginally below. The near-misses reflect narrow effect sizes rather than classification failure.
By architecture family: Qwen (4/4) and LLaMA (1/1) yield $\Kempirical{\geq}2$ at nominal significance; OLMo (1/1), Mistral (1/1), and Gemma (1/1) yield $\Kempirical{=}1$; Pythia exhibits a scale-dependent split (1.4B: $\Kempirical{=}2$, 6.9B: $\Kempirical{=}1$).
LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms $\Kempirical{=}2$ (0v1 $\auc = 0.9225$, 1v2 $\auc = 0.6123\;[0.603,\,0.622]$; CI lower bound above $\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).
The 1v2 spread due to generation mode ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp within Qwen) exceeds the spread across $\Kempirical{=}2$ continuation models ($\Delta = 1.9$\,pp).

\paragraph{Scale and instruction-tuning invariance.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ persists from 1.5B through 8B: Qwen-7B (1v2 $\auc = 0.6168$ $[0.608, 0.626]$) and LLaMA-3.1-8B (1v2 $\auc = 0.6123$) both exceed $\theta$, despite progressively reduced 0v1 signal (0.9638 and 0.9225 vs.\ $> 0.99$ at $\leq$1.5B).
A base-vs.-instruct ablation at 1.5B likewise yields $\Kempirical = 2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.626$), confirming the boundary is not an instruction-tuning artifact.

